---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-mailchimp';
const title = 'Postscript vs Mailchimp for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Shopify SMS, email access, campaign controls and contribution economics with proposed acceptance tests for Postscript and Mailchimp.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which should a Shopify merchant evaluate?",
    "answer": "Evaluate Postscript for a Shopify SMS program and Mailchimp for email and Shopify lifecycle automation, with SMS where eligible. Choose only after testing your account, destination countries, data mapping and operating costs."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Postscript offer email?",
    "answer": "Postscript has announced Email and describes shared profiles and email/SMS flows. Its access FAQ still describes waitlist and cohort onboarding. Confirm activation and required features in your account before retiring an existing sender."
  },
  {
    "question": "How does Mailchimp connect to Shopify?",
    "answer": "Mailchimp for Shopify syncs store data and customer events for marketing automation. Subscribed SMS contact sync requires an approved SMS Marketing program. Verify audience mapping, consent history, timing and account capabilities before sending."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are transactional messages marketing permission?",
    "answer": "No. Keep service notices and promotions separate. Verify the permitted purpose, channel and destination rules before sending; an order event does not establish promotional consent."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can either run a limited drop?",
    "answer": "Use the proposed fixtures below to verify inventory freshness, variant eligibility, purchase exits and queue cancellation. No supplier is declared a winner from these fictional examples."
  },
  {
    "question": "Must I add Sequenzy?",
    "answer": "No. Assign email to one approved owner. Retain an existing provider or assess another only for a demonstrated requirement; confirm Postscript Email access and Mailchimp account eligibility before choosing."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Request current quotes for the same eligible audience, countries, traffic and peak calendar. Include platform, message segments, carrier charges, email, implementation and support costs, then compare holdout-adjusted contribution."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can providers overlap during migration?",
    "answer": "Only with explicit ownership per purpose and channel, shared suppression checks and retired old queues. Test replay deduplication and rollback before allowing production sends."
  },
  {
    "question": "What is a purchase-exit acceptance test?",
    "answer": "Place an order after scheduling a promotion, replay and delay its event, and verify the matching identity exits every applicable promotional queue before dispatch. Escalate ambiguous identities for review."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-14">
    <nav><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Postscript vs Mailchimp</nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl">
      <p class="badge">Shopify campaign evaluation</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Postscript vs Mailchimp for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl">Choose around your channel ownership, Shopify data and campaign controls. Neither supplier earns a ranking from the fictional merchant fixtures on this page.</p>
    </header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Supplier scope and positive conditional fits</h2>
      <p>Postscript is a candidate for a Shopify SMS program that needs list capture, segmentation and campaign automation. Its <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/launch">launch page</a> announces Email, and its <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/email">Email page</a> describes shared profiles and email/SMS flows. The access FAQ still describes a waitlist with cohort onboarding. Verify activation, migration support and the exact flow controls in your account before assigning email ownership. An announcement is not an entitlement.</p>
      <p>Mailchimp is a candidate for a team that wants email campaigns and Shopify lifecycle automation with SMS where its account is eligible. Its <a class="link" href="https://mailchimp.com/help/mailchimp-shopify-syncing/">Shopify syncing documentation</a> describes customer, product, order and customer-event data for marketing automations. Verify the connected store, audience, field mapping and event timing before using a lifecycle trigger or purchase exit.</p>
      <p>Mailchimp offers SMS marketing, subject to <a class="link" href="https://mailchimp.com/help/about-sms-marketing/">availability and account conditions</a>. Confirm your business and recipient countries, eligible paid plan, sender type, credits and feature access in the account. Its <a class="link" href="https://mailchimp.com/help/set-up-sms-marketing/">setup documentation</a> requires application and approval for each sending number. MMS has separate country and plan limits; do not infer MMS availability from SMS coverage.</p>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://mailchimp.com/help/manage-mailchimp-shopify/">Shopify integration guide</a> requires an approved SMS Marketing program to sync subscribed SMS contacts. Reconcile historical consent, sync dates and suppression precedence before enabling sends. Test abandoned-cart, post-purchase and other lifecycle events against real account capabilities; a Shopify connection does not prove every desired trigger, exit or transactional notification is configured. Keep receipts with their approved owner until a separate service-message path passes testing.</p>
      <p>Supplier documentation reviewed November 25, 2026. These are product descriptions, not account tests. Evaluate each provider positively where its activated features meet your requirements; keep an existing email sender or consider another provider only for a demonstrated gap. No additional stack is mandatory.</p>
      <h2>Use the same acceptance contract</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Postscript evaluation</th><th>Mailchimp evaluation</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Permission and identity</td><td>Map capture records to Shopify and channel profiles</td><td>Verify subscribed-contact sync and suppression precedence</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Email ownership</td><td>Verify cohort access and required flows</td><td>Verify activated email features and Shopify mapping</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SMS campaigns</td><td>Test segments, queue controls and destination support</td><td>Test activated SMS, sender registration and destination support</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Service messages</td><td>Confirm the required integration and owner</td><td>Verify service-message ownership separately from marketing flows</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <h2>Fourteen fictional proposed merchant fixtures</h2>
      <p>All names, quantities, thresholds and schedules below are invented acceptance inputs. They are not customer stories, observed outcomes, supplier benchmarks or promises of built-in functionality. Run each relevant fixture against both candidate accounts using test recipients; record pass, fail or an explicit implementation dependency.</p>
      <h3>Sauce Lab: Batch-number scarcity</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Seed a fictional batch with 200 bottles left. Permit one launch and at most one scarcity text only while fresh inventory supports the claim; a sold-out update cancels queued scarcity.</p>
      <h3>Ember Ceramics: Kiln waitlist fairness</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Seed 4,200 fictional requests for an 80-piece kiln. Preserve timestamp priority, deduplicate identities, and never promise that an invitation reserves stock. A retry must not jump the queue.</p>
      <h3>Ridge Run Coffee: Subscription collision</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Seed active, paused and cancelled subscriptions. Suppress active subscribers from the non-subscriber flash offer, and reconcile a status change before dispatch rather than relying on a stale export.</p>
      <h3>Cipher Audio: Full-price launch</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Propose a teaser email two weeks before launch and an SMS only to eligible engaged browsers. Require approval for any discount change; compare full-price and incentive cohorts without asserting a margin result.</p>
      <h3>Patchwork Denim: One-of-one style matching</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Seed one unique garment and two interested shoppers. Match explicit style preferences, recheck variant availability, and retire the remaining listing messages after purchase.</p>
      <h3>Arena Energy: Athlete collaboration</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Propose flavor-drop timing by SMS and a shaker offer in email. Require separate approved copy and eligibility rules so a timing text cannot silently inherit an incentive or an unsupported athlete claim.</p>
      <h3>Loom & Frame: Edition sell-through</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Seed collectors with three prior orders and a disclosed edition size. Propose a halfway-sold trigger only after reconciling paid orders, cancellations and returns; cancel it if the threshold no longer holds.</p>
      <h3>Driftwood Surf Co: Seasonal restock</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Match a boardshort variant waitlist to available sizes. Treat swell-forecast content as editorial context, never inventory evidence; expire old requests and suppress purchasers before the restock message.</p>
      <h3>Noir Collective: VIP calendar</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Propose a Thursday 6pm release with VIP access two hours earlier. Resolve recipient timezone, overlapping promotions and frequency caps before approval; a delayed job must not send an expired early-access link.</p>
      <h3>SoleDrop ATX: Size-run priority</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Seed a sneaker release with size-specific allocations. Propose general access after 70% sell-through only if remaining sizes justify it. Test concurrent purchases and queue retirement without claiming faster sales or fewer unsubscribes.</p>
      <h3>Festival Threads Co: Tour routing</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Seed city and venue-timezone preferences. Propose at most three texts per city, reject stale tour dates and missing location evidence, and require renewed approval when a venue or local schedule changes.</p>
      <h3>Vinyl Vault Records: Collector eligibility</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Seed a disclosed pressing quantity and a collector tier of two prior orders or a proposed $400 historical value threshold. Define returns and currency treatment; no tier overrides waitlist permission or stock truth.</p>
      <h3>Bloom & Wick: Gift deadline calendar</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Propose an August holiday plan separating gift bundles from self-purchase scent education. Use current fulfillment cutoffs and destination evidence; cancel deadline claims when warehouse capacity changes.</p>
      <h3>Peak Trail Gear: Clearance holdout</h3>
      <p>Proposed fixture: Propose a 15% offer for a defined bargain-sensitive quartile and a matched holdout. Exclude full-price loyalists by the approved rule, then measure contribution without inventing a completed test result.</p>
      <h2>Permission, identity and order truth before dispatch</h2>
      <p>Store channel, purpose, disclosure version, source and timestamp with every permission record. A purchase, phone number or email opt-in alone does not grant SMS marketing permission. Keep service notices separate from promotions. Have the responsible reviewer confirm destination-specific requirements, quiet hours and sender handling; do not treat this page as legal advice.</p>
      <p>Resolve Shopify customer IDs, normalized phone numbers and email identities without merging unrelated household members or shared numbers. Quarantine ambiguous matches. Reconcile opt-outs, complaints, deletion requests and holdouts at scheduling and immediately before dispatch. Test recipient timezone and daylight-saving changes; pause uncertain records rather than guessing.</p>
      <p>Use Shopify order and inventory truth, including cancellations, refunds, variant reservations and delayed events. Place an order after a promotion is queued and verify purchase exits across all applicable promotional channels before sending. Deliver duplicate and out-of-order webhooks and replay a failed worker: use stable event, customer, purpose and campaign keys so a retry cannot create a second message. A stale purchase feed blocks the affected campaign.</p>
      <h2>Approval, handoff and rollback</h2>
      <p>Give one named operator ownership of each channel and purpose, a second reviewer responsibility for copy, claims, segment versions and incentives, and support an escalation path for replies and failed sends. Record approval and expiry with the campaign. Test pausing, cancelling and retiring every old scheduled job, flow and retry queue, not just disabling the visible campaign.</p>
      <p>During a handoff, export and reconcile permission evidence, suppression history, identity mappings, purchase exits and pending work. Shadow-test without sending, then use a small approved cohort. Freeze the old owner before activating the new one and reconcile delivery acknowledgements before replaying uncertain work. Rollback pauses the new owner, retires its queues, restores the last approved mapping and rechecks suppressions before any old sender resumes. Never reactivate both blindly.</p>
      <h2>Matched hypothetical contribution economics</h2>
      <p>Use the same eligible audience, products, calendar, destinations and observation window for each candidate. Randomize a comparable holdout and reconcile orders outside platform attribution. Report contribution per eligible customer, including those who received no message, rather than comparing unequal revenue-per-send denominators.</p>
      <p>Hypothetical arithmetic only: 1,000 eligible customers produce $10,000 in net sales after discounts and returns. Subtract $4,000 product costs, $1,000 fulfillment/payment costs, $300 platform allocation, $200 message/carrier costs and $100 support cost: contribution is $4,400, or $4.40 per eligible customer. A matched 1,000-customer holdout with $4,000 contribution gives a hypothetical difference of $400, or $0.40 per eligible customer. This is not a supplier result. Discounts and returns are already in net sales and must not be subtracted twice.</p>
      <p>Apply identical cost definitions to Postscript and Mailchimp, adding migration labor, email costs, add-ons and any implementation required to pass the fixtures. Obtain current written quotes for peak volume, message encoding/segments, destination and carrier charges, account limits, minimums and overages. Holdout uncertainty, stock imbalance and event loss can invalidate a comparison; record those limits before scaling.</p>
      <h2>Decision after the pilot</h2>
      <p>Choose Postscript if its activated Shopify SMS controls, any confirmed Email access and operating economics satisfy the approved jobs. Choose Mailchimp if its activated channels, Shopify lifecycle mapping, notification boundaries and operating economics satisfy the same jobs. If either fails a critical permission, purchase-exit or queue-retirement test, hold that workflow until repaired. A successful fixture establishes only the tested configuration, not universal suitability.</p>
      <p>Continue with <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent records</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a>, or inspect the <a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps/mailchimp">Mailchimp</a> profiles.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Postscript vs Mailchimp FAQ" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
